WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.001
-Our next guest represents

00:00:02.001 --> 00:00:04.136
California's 28th
Congressional district.

00:00:04.136 --> 00:00:06.439
His number one "New York Times"
best selling book

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:09.075
"Midnight in Washington:"
How We Almost Lost Our Democracy

00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:10.576
and Still Could"
is on sale now.

00:00:10.576 --> 00:00:13.946
Please welcome back to the show
Congressman Adam Schiff.

00:00:13.946 --> 00:00:15.415
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.415 --> 00:00:21.354 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:27.293 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:29.395
How are you?
-I'm good. I'm good.

00:00:29.395 --> 00:00:31.297
-This is a lovely place to
see you.

00:00:31.297 --> 00:00:33.366
The last time I saw you
was not where I expected

00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:36.736
to see a congressman.
I was at my inlaws' house.

00:00:36.736 --> 00:00:38.070
-Yes.
-I was sitting on the steps

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.807
with my son.
-And I was lost.

00:00:40.807 --> 00:00:43.642
-Yeah.
-And I drove up to a house

00:00:43.642 --> 00:00:46.312
with my cousin and knocked on
the door. And it was you.

00:00:46.312 --> 00:00:48.447
-Yeah.
-And then I was even more lost.

00:00:48.447 --> 00:00:50.850
-Yeah, and your cousin
stepped out of the car.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.553
And I was trying to help him
and give him directions.

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:56.122
And we had met.
And you stepped out.

00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:59.225
And I will admit based on the
role you often serve

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:01.628
in government I thought I was
about to get served a subpoena.

00:01:01.628 --> 00:01:02.896
[ Laughter ]

00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:04.463
Because in movies
they always trick you.

00:01:04.463 --> 00:01:05.798
Always start talking about
something else

00:01:05.798 --> 00:01:06.900
and then they hand it to you.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.100
And I'm like, son of a gun.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:12.305
-Now that you mention it --
-Oh, no, no, no.

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:15.375
-So you're very honest about
a lot of things in this book.

00:01:15.375 --> 00:01:17.744
Including how you feel about
some of your colleagues.

00:01:17.744 --> 00:01:19.412
Including Kevin McCarthy
who we talked about earlier

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:21.314
in the show.

00:01:21.314 --> 00:01:23.649
How do you feel -- I should --
I should point out,

00:01:23.649 --> 00:01:25.651
maybe not the biggest fan
of Kevin McCarthy.

00:01:25.651 --> 00:01:28.188
And how did you feel watching
his eight and a half hour

00:01:28.188 --> 00:01:30.090
speech the other night.
-It was torture.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:34.060
-Yeah.
-It was my first moron-a-thon.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.930
[ Laughter ]

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:38.731
[ Applause ]

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:42.701
And I kept thinking if this is
his way of punishing Paul Gosar

00:01:42.701 --> 00:01:45.204
why make the rest of us suffer.
-Right have to sit through it.

00:01:45.204 --> 00:01:49.075
-It was rambling,
it was incoherent.

00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:50.643
And the strange thing
about it is,

00:01:50.643 --> 00:01:53.612
if you're going to filibuster
something, do you really want to

00:01:53.612 --> 00:01:58.083
filibuster early childhood
education, quality child care,

00:01:58.083 --> 00:02:00.119
hearing aids for seniors?

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:03.289
The most vigorous effort to
combat climate change?

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:05.725
Is that really what you want to
try to filibuster?

00:02:05.725 --> 00:02:08.827
-Well, I wonder why it
is he would do that.

00:02:08.827 --> 00:02:12.031
Because Build Back Better,
the elements of it,

00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:14.566
are very popular,
these are things people want.

00:02:14.566 --> 00:02:16.569
And yet the Republican Party
does seem like there is

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:19.505
some sort of electoral advantage
to running against it.

00:02:19.505 --> 00:02:21.875
Do you feel like
people are going to

00:02:21.875 --> 00:02:23.042
get excited about this bill?

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:24.344
And do you feel like
it's something

00:02:24.344 --> 00:02:26.379
the Democratic Party is going to
be able to run on

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:28.414
as they approach the mid-terms
and, maybe,

00:02:28.414 --> 00:02:30.316
people are not particularly
optimistic about

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:32.451
insofar as how your party's
going to do.

00:02:32.451 --> 00:02:35.455
-I think we're going to have
the most comprehensive

00:02:35.455 --> 00:02:38.657
positive agenda any Congress
had to run on.

00:02:38.657 --> 00:02:40.426
The combination of
the rescue plan,

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:42.862
the infrastructure bill,
Build Back Better,

00:02:42.862 --> 00:02:47.100
will be, essentially, the new
deal for this new generation,

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:50.202
lifting half the nation's
children out of poverty,

00:02:50.202 --> 00:02:52.805
attacking the problem
of climate change,

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:56.109
helping all Americans with
prescription drug prices,

00:02:56.109 --> 00:02:58.244
helping young people
afford college,

00:02:58.244 --> 00:03:01.014
helping others get a good job.

00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:02.715
And, you know,
the challenge will be,

00:03:02.715 --> 00:03:05.785
okay, what are we going to focus
on when we go around the country

00:03:05.785 --> 00:03:09.221
and tell people what we did
because it's so expansive?

00:03:09.221 --> 00:03:11.924
But I think it gives us a
real fighting chance to buck

00:03:11.924 --> 00:03:15.928
that mid-term trend,
where the party in power

00:03:15.928 --> 00:03:17.429
in the White House loses seats.

00:03:17.429 --> 00:03:19.398
And we are going to have to
defeat that trend.

00:03:19.398 --> 00:03:22.735
Because the idea that someone
with the kind of lack

00:03:22.735 --> 00:03:27.373
of morality or ethics
like Kevin McCarthy

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:31.009
could ever go near the Speaker's
office is just terrifying.

00:03:31.009 --> 00:03:34.213
-You know, I don't know
the history of the new deal.

00:03:34.213 --> 00:03:36.782
I obviously know how it was
received once it was passed.

00:03:36.782 --> 00:03:40.486
But how much do you think
what's being passed now

00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:44.357
is suffering from how fraught
the negotiations have been

00:03:44.357 --> 00:03:45.691
sort of within
the Democratic Party?

00:03:45.691 --> 00:03:48.560
Because it does feel like every
part of it is just being

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.262
dragged across the finish line.

00:03:50.262 --> 00:03:52.265
And, you know because
of the cable news cycle

00:03:52.265 --> 00:03:53.999
and talking about how difficult
it is to get it passed

00:03:53.999 --> 00:03:57.069
I think people may think there's
something wrong to it.

00:03:57.069 --> 00:03:58.871
-I think
the lead up to the passage

00:03:58.871 --> 00:04:00.706
of the infrastructure bill,
you know,

00:04:00.706 --> 00:04:02.841
had Democrats
fighting Democrats.

00:04:02.841 --> 00:04:05.378
And that was just
singly destructive.

00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:08.047
When we got to the Build Back
Better there was none of that.

00:04:08.047 --> 00:04:10.683
I think we realized just we
can't afford to be fighting

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:12.184
amongst ourselves.

00:04:12.184 --> 00:04:15.855
You know, the answer to the
challenge of getting more

00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:20.993
out of the Senate is we need
more Democrats in the Senate.

00:04:20.993 --> 00:04:25.498
We need to -- if we can't get
everything we want

00:04:25.498 --> 00:04:28.968
with an evenly divided Senate
that's 50/50,

00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:32.037
we need more Democrats in
the Senate to be able to

00:04:32.037 --> 00:04:33.773
build on
what we've already done.

00:04:33.773 --> 00:04:36.175
It's not to go after each other.

00:04:36.175 --> 00:04:39.679
-So you were the first
impeachment manager.

00:04:39.679 --> 00:04:42.015
Now there's the January 6th
investigation.

00:04:42.015 --> 00:04:43.282
We're going to come up,
you know --

00:04:43.282 --> 00:04:44.384
obviously we're going to come up

00:04:44.384 --> 00:04:46.752
on the year anniversary
pretty soon.

00:04:46.752 --> 00:04:50.589
What is -- what are you hopeful
to accomplish from this?

00:04:50.589 --> 00:04:53.459
Because one thing
that makes me depressed

00:04:53.459 --> 00:04:55.627
is it doesn't seem like
anyone involved in that

00:04:55.627 --> 00:04:57.596
has paid any price at all.

00:04:57.596 --> 00:05:01.734
And it's doubtful based on how
polling is going right now

00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:05.771
for Republicans,
that it is going to ever

00:05:05.771 --> 00:05:07.072
have a cost for them.

00:05:07.072 --> 00:05:09.441
Is that -- is there any outcome
here where people

00:05:09.441 --> 00:05:13.345
don't look at what you find
and it's just a partisan result,

00:05:13.345 --> 00:05:15.882
like some people and go, "Oh,
that's what I always expected."

00:05:15.882 --> 00:05:18.951
Or other people look at it and
say, "This is a witch hunt?"

00:05:18.951 --> 00:05:23.556
-Look, I think all we can do
is expose all the malefactors,

00:05:23.556 --> 00:05:25.258
follow the evidence
wherever it leads,

00:05:25.258 --> 00:05:26.726
tell the American people
the story of

00:05:26.726 --> 00:05:30.530
what went in to January 6th, all
the planning that went into it,

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:32.865
who was behind it
in terms of the money.

00:05:32.865 --> 00:05:35.701
What Donald Trump was doing,
what he was not doing

00:05:35.701 --> 00:05:38.170
at the time that the Capitol
was being attacked.

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:43.309
And make the case publicly,
expose all the wrong doing,

00:05:43.309 --> 00:05:45.511
and at the same time
make sure that we

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:48.180
don't lose sight of our
legislative agenda.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:51.250
And we have to hope that there
is still enough American people

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:54.319
with an open mind that they will
follow the hearings

00:05:54.319 --> 00:05:55.588
that we're going to have
in public,

00:05:55.588 --> 00:05:59.391
that they'll be interested in
what we conclude.

00:05:59.391 --> 00:06:02.261
We are working in a very
non-partisan way,

00:06:02.261 --> 00:06:04.030
Democrats and Republicans
on the subcommittee.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:07.333
It's one of the few bright spots
in the Congress right now.

00:06:07.333 --> 00:06:10.135
But I think
what you're pointing to is

00:06:10.135 --> 00:06:11.971
one of the overarching
challenges we have

00:06:11.971 --> 00:06:14.172
which is people don't get all
their information

00:06:14.172 --> 00:06:15.741
from the same places.

00:06:15.741 --> 00:06:19.678
Of all the Facebook
whistle-blower allegations

00:06:19.678 --> 00:06:23.349
that I found the most troubling
was at Facebook they created

00:06:23.349 --> 00:06:25.084
a Republican woman.

00:06:25.084 --> 00:06:27.419
They gave her only a few
attributes.

00:06:27.419 --> 00:06:31.390
She was "Fox" viewing, she was
Christian, and Republican.

00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:34.894
And their algorithms led her
straight to QAnon.

00:06:34.894 --> 00:06:37.329
And as long as that's the case
and people are led down

00:06:37.329 --> 00:06:42.068
these tragic conspiratorial
rabbit holes

00:06:42.068 --> 00:06:45.237
we're going to have this
horrible divide.

00:06:45.237 --> 00:06:50.009
So we're going to have to try to
address that challenge.

00:06:50.009 --> 00:06:52.912
-Steve Bannon who seems to
commit a lot of crimes

00:06:52.912 --> 00:06:56.515
but not get any better at it
while he goes,

00:06:56.515 --> 00:06:58.651
he was sort of called
in front of the house,

00:06:58.651 --> 00:07:00.786
basically wouldn't
turn over documents.

00:07:00.786 --> 00:07:02.188
Correct me
if I get any of this wrong.

00:07:02.188 --> 00:07:04.590
And now he has been arrested.

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:09.027
Do you think this is going to
end up with him -- I don't know,

00:07:09.027 --> 00:07:11.063
like, being cooperative
with the committee to

00:07:11.063 --> 00:07:13.298
try to stay out of prison?

00:07:13.298 --> 00:07:18.104
Or do you think he will continue
to be sort of stuck in his ways?

00:07:18.104 --> 00:07:22.240
-I don't know. And, Seth,
I look at one thing

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:25.878
as sort of a microcosm,
just shows you what a consummate

00:07:25.878 --> 00:07:29.248
group of grifters were running
the country.

00:07:29.248 --> 00:07:32.951
A man runs for President on a
platform of building a wall

00:07:32.951 --> 00:07:35.554
that he says Mexico
is going to pay for.

00:07:35.554 --> 00:07:39.725
-I don't think he said that, no.
-Of course.

00:07:39.725 --> 00:07:42.361
He becomes President.
He doesn't build a wall.

00:07:42.361 --> 00:07:44.730
Mexico doesn't pay for it.
-Right.

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:47.733
-His cronies then start a fund
raising money

00:07:47.733 --> 00:07:50.703
from his own supporters to
purportedly build a wall.

00:07:50.703 --> 00:07:53.238
They steal it.
Like Steve Bannon.

00:07:53.238 --> 00:07:56.908
They are indicted for stealing
money from his own people.

00:07:56.908 --> 00:07:59.878
And then he pardons them.

00:07:59.878 --> 00:08:03.916
And they're just a bunch
of crooks and grifters.

00:08:03.916 --> 00:08:06.885
But you can understand
why Steve Bannon believed

00:08:06.885 --> 00:08:09.789
that he was above the law,
because for four years he was.

00:08:09.789 --> 00:08:12.624
But now we have a justice
department that believes

00:08:12.624 --> 00:08:14.093
no one is above the law.

00:08:14.093 --> 00:08:17.996
And I think the effect of this
prosecution, whatever he does,

00:08:17.996 --> 00:08:22.000
has already had a very important
impact on other witnesses

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:25.071
who have decided to cooperate
because they don't want to

00:08:25.071 --> 00:08:27.106
be prosecuted like Steve Bannon.

00:08:27.106 --> 00:08:30.843
-There's -- you talk about
January 6th and your colleagues

00:08:30.843 --> 00:08:33.412
from across the aisle were
concerned about your wellbeing.

00:08:33.412 --> 00:08:35.948
Like that was a very harrowing
day in real time, I think,

00:08:35.948 --> 00:08:38.117
that when something like that
happens,

00:08:38.117 --> 00:08:42.187
people do fall back to their
best humanity, right?

00:08:42.187 --> 00:08:44.790
Concern about people they maybe
don't agree with all the time.

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:47.660
Has is it been depressing to you
that that moment of,

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:50.062
maybe, seeing the best in people
you don't agree with

00:08:50.062 --> 00:08:52.764
has faded away?

00:08:52.764 --> 00:08:55.601
-It is. I was there, as you
know, for the 6th.

00:08:55.601 --> 00:09:00.806
And as they were breaking
the doors and windows to get in,

00:09:00.806 --> 00:09:03.575
I had a number of Republicans
come up to me and say,

00:09:03.575 --> 00:09:05.278
"You can't let them see you."

00:09:05.278 --> 00:09:06.646
One of them said,
"I know these people.

00:09:06.646 --> 00:09:07.713
I can talk to these people.

00:09:07.713 --> 00:09:09.248
I can talk my way
through these people,

00:09:09.248 --> 00:09:10.382
you can't let them see you."

00:09:10.382 --> 00:09:11.950
You're in
a whole different category.

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:14.553
And I was -- you know, I was
touched that they were concerned

00:09:14.553 --> 00:09:16.122
about my safety.

00:09:16.122 --> 00:09:18.223
But immediately that thought was
followed by another,

00:09:18.223 --> 00:09:20.826
that if they hadn't been lying
about the election,

00:09:20.826 --> 00:09:22.494
I wouldn't need to be
worried about my safety.

00:09:22.494 --> 00:09:23.896
And none of us would.

00:09:23.896 --> 00:09:27.633
And when we left the chamber
and came back

00:09:27.633 --> 00:09:30.602
after the police put down
that insurrection

00:09:30.602 --> 00:09:32.604
these same people
were pushing --

00:09:32.604 --> 00:09:33.906
still pushing the big lie.

00:09:33.906 --> 00:09:36.041
Still voting to overturn
the election.

00:09:36.041 --> 00:09:38.711
And whatever good will

00:09:38.711 --> 00:09:42.915
I felt about their concern
for me evaporated.

00:09:42.915 --> 00:09:45.484
We had a chance
after that terrible tragedy

00:09:45.484 --> 00:09:47.253
to turn a corner as a country.

00:09:47.253 --> 00:09:50.389
But they have just -- these --
What I call insurrectionists

00:09:50.389 --> 00:09:54.059
in suits and ties have just
doubled down on the big lie.

00:09:54.059 --> 00:09:56.562
And they're persuading millions
of Americans

00:09:56.562 --> 00:09:57.796
that we can't trust
our elections.

00:09:57.796 --> 00:09:59.665
And if you persuade
millions of people

00:09:59.665 --> 00:10:01.333
that we can't trust our
elections to decide

00:10:01.333 --> 00:10:04.837
who should govern,
then what is left but violence?

00:10:04.837 --> 00:10:08.974
And this is -- this is why
things are so precarious.

00:10:08.974 --> 00:10:11.810
But the answer has to be
not despair.

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:15.748
The answer has to be engagement,
even greater engagement

00:10:15.748 --> 00:10:17.783
to get us through this.

00:10:17.783 --> 00:10:20.118
One of the reasons I titled the
book "Midnight in Washington"

00:10:20.118 --> 00:10:22.521
is midnight is the darkest hour
of every day.

00:10:22.521 --> 00:10:24.690
But it's also a hopeful time.

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:27.359
Because we know that what
follows is light.

00:10:27.359 --> 00:10:29.862
And there are millions and
millions more Americans

00:10:29.862 --> 00:10:32.297
who love and cherish our
democracy

00:10:32.297 --> 00:10:35.000
than those who are determined at
the moment to tear it down.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:36.402
And they will
get us through this.

00:10:36.402 --> 00:10:37.603
-Well, I'm just going to
say this.

00:10:37.603 --> 00:10:41.640
It sounds like a really weird
place to work.

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:42.808
Thank you so much.

00:10:42.808 --> 00:10:44.643
That's Congressman Adam Schiff,
everybody.

00:10:44.643 --> 00:10:45.945
"Midnight in Washington"
is out now.

